\title{The Only Cost Is Height:\\
A Unit-Cost Ultrametric Functor from Valuation Depth to Tropical Trees}
\author{The Aristotle Collaboration}
\date{2026-06-16}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a single, deceptively simple inequality---the \emph{unit-cost
ultrametric law} $\depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y) + 1$---and show
that it governs the accumulation of complexity across three superficially
unrelated settings: tropical/valuation arithmetic, function composition, and
$p$-adic Hensel/Newton lifting. We package the law as a \emph{depth carrier} and
analyze how complexity propagates through arbitrary binary \emph{combination
trees}. Our central structural result is that the evaluated depth of a tree is
bounded by its maximum leaf depth plus its \emph{height}---never directly by its
leaf count. From this we derive five results (C1--C5). (C2) The unit constant
$1$ is the unique least Lipschitz constant valid across all depth carriers,
pinning the bridge's constant intrinsically. (C1) Balanced reassociation meets
the logarithmic bound $\maxLeafDepth + \clog \numLeaves\rceil$, whereas an
explicit caterpillar witness violates it; the balanced-versus-caterpillar gap on
$2^n$ leaves is exponential ($n$ versus $2^n - 1$). (C4) The height bound extends
verbatim to composition trees, giving balanced composition of $2^n$ depth-$d$
maps an exact depth of $d + n$. (C5) The $k$-fold quadratic-doubling tree has
evaluated depth exactly $k$ and certifies $p$-adic precision exactly $2^k$,
recovering the classical exponential-precision Hensel certificate. (C3) For
strict (idempotent) carriers the height overhead vanishes.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A great many constructions are \emph{iterated binary combinations}: build a final
object by repeatedly fusing two intermediates. Sums, products, parallel
reductions, compound functions, and successively refined numerical approximations
all share this shape. In each case there is a notion of \emph{complexity}---degree,
valuation, $p$-adic precision, critical-path length---and combining two objects
increases it by a small, fixed amount. We isolate the cleanest accounting of that
increase, a \emph{tropical} (max-plus) inequality with unit slack,
\[
  \depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y) + 1,
\]
which is precisely a nonarchimedean / ultrametric triangle inequality. Our thesis
is that this one law is the common denominator of tropical valuation growth,
composition-depth growth, and Hensel/Newton precision doubling, and that the
propagation of complexity through an arbitrary combination process is controlled
by a single invariant: its \emph{height}, never directly its size.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{C2:} a constant $c$ validates the law across all carriers iff
    $c \ge 1$; the bridge's Lipschitz constant is intrinsically $1$.
  \item \textbf{C1:} balanced reassociation meets the logarithmic bound; a
    caterpillar violates the naive bound; the gap on $2^n$ leaves is exponential.
  \item \textbf{C4:} the height bound transfers to composition trees; balanced
    composition of $2^n$ depth-$d$ maps has depth exactly $d + n$.
  \item \textbf{C5:} the $k$-fold doubling tree has depth $k$ and precision
    $2^k$, recovering exponential-precision Hensel lifting.
  \item \textbf{C3:} strict (idempotent) carriers incur zero height overhead.
\end{itemize}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\Nat$ denotes the natural numbers and $\clog m\rceil$ the binary
ceiling-logarithm, with $\clog 2^n\rceil = n$.

\begin{definition}[Depth carrier]
A \emph{depth carrier} is a tuple $X = (K, \depth, \mathrm{add})$ with a type $K$,
a function $\depth : K \to \Nat$, and a binary operation
$\mathrm{add} : K \to K \to K$ (written $x \op y$), satisfying the
\emph{unit-cost ultrametric law}
\[
  \depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y) + 1 \qquad \text{for all } x, y \in K.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Witness carrier]
The \emph{witness carrier} $W$ has $K = \Nat$, $\depth = \mathrm{id}$, and
$x \op y = \mathrm{unitCostAdd}(x,y) := \max(x,y) + 1$. Its law holds with
equality everywhere; in particular $\mathrm{unitCostAdd}(0,0) = 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Strict carrier]
A depth carrier $X$ is \emph{strict} if its combination is idempotent and obeys
$\depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y)$ (zero slack).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Operation tree]
For a type $K$, the type $\mathrm{OpTree}\,K$ of \emph{combination trees} is
generated by $\mathrm{leaf} : K \to \mathrm{OpTree}\,K$ and
$\mathrm{node} : \mathrm{OpTree}\,K \to \mathrm{OpTree}\,K \to \mathrm{OpTree}\,K$.
We define, recursively,
\begin{align*}
  \height(\mathrm{leaf}\,k) &= 0, & \height(\mathrm{node}\,l\,r) &= \max(\height l, \height r) + 1,\\
  \numLeaves(\mathrm{leaf}\,k) &= 1, & \numLeaves(\mathrm{node}\,l\,r) &= \numLeaves l + \numLeaves r,\\
  \maxLeafDepth(\mathrm{leaf}\,k) &= \depth k, & \maxLeafDepth(\mathrm{node}\,l\,r) &= \max(\maxLeafDepth l, \maxLeafDepth r),\\
  \eval(\mathrm{leaf}\,k) &= k, & \eval(\mathrm{node}\,l\,r) &= \mathrm{op}(\eval l, \eval r).
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical families]
For $k : K$, the \emph{balanced} tree
$\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,0 = \mathrm{leaf}\,k$,
$\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,(n{+}1) = \mathrm{node}(\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,n)(\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,n)$
has height $n$, $2^n$ leaves, $\maxLeafDepth = \depth k$. The \emph{caterpillar}
$\mathrm{caterpillar}\,k\,0 = \mathrm{leaf}\,k$,
$\mathrm{caterpillar}\,k\,(n{+}1) = \mathrm{node}(\mathrm{caterpillar}\,k\,n)(\mathrm{leaf}\,k)$
has height $n$, $n+1$ leaves, $\maxLeafDepth = \depth k$.
\end{definition}

\section{The core height bound}

\begin{theorem}[Main height bound]\label{thm:main}
For every depth carrier $X$ and every tree $t$,
\[
  \depth(\eval t\ X.\mathrm{add}) \le \maxLeafDepth\,\depth\,t + \height t.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $t$. The leaf case is equality at height $0$. For
$t = \mathrm{node}\,l\,r$ with $M = \maxLeafDepth t$, the unit-cost law and the
inductive hypotheses give
\[
  \depth(\eval t) \le \max(\depth\eval l, \depth\eval r) + 1
  \le \max(M + \height l, M + \height r) + 1 = M + \height t.
\]
The toll $+1$ is paid once per level of the longest root-to-leaf chain only.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Constant leaves]\label{cor:const}
If every leaf of $t$ has depth $b$, then
$\depth(\eval t\ X.\mathrm{add}) \le b + \height t$.
\end{corollary}

\section{C2: the unit cost is the unique least Lipschitz constant}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:lip}
For $c \in \Nat$,
\[
  \bigl(\forall X,\ \forall x,y,\ \depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y) + c\bigr)
  \iff 1 \le c.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) At $W$ with $x=y=0$: $\depth(0 \op 0) = 1 \le c$.
($\Leftarrow$) For $1 \le c$, each carrier already satisfies the law with $+1 \le +c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:least}
$1$ is the least element of
$\{c \in \Nat \mid \forall X, \forall x,y,\ \depth(x \op y) \le \max(\depth x, \depth y) + c\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Membership is the defining law; minimality is the forward direction of
Theorem~\ref{thm:lip}.
\end{proof}

\noindent The constant $1$ is thus intrinsic: $c = 0$ is refuted because
combining two depth-zero atoms genuinely produces a depth-one object.

\section{C1: height, not leaf count, is the true cost}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:baleval}
$\eval(\mathrm{balanced}\,b\,n)\ \mathrm{unitCostAdd} = b + n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cateval}
$\eval(\mathrm{caterpillar}\,b\,n)\ \mathrm{unitCostAdd} = b + n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both by induction: a balanced step yields $\max(b{+}n,b{+}n)+1 = b+(n{+}1)$; a
caterpillar step yields $\max(b{+}n,b)+1 = b+(n{+}1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Balanced meets the log bound]\label{thm:balbound}
For every depth carrier $X$, $k : K$, and $n$,
\[
  \depth(\eval(\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,n)\ X.\mathrm{add})
  \le \depth k + \clog \numLeaves(\mathrm{balanced}\,k\,n)\rceil.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main}; with $\numLeaves = 2^n$ and $\clog 2^n\rceil = n$
the bound is $\depth k + n$, matching the height bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Naive log bound fails]\label{thm:fail}
There exist $X$ and $t$ with
$\maxLeafDepth\,\depth\,t + \clog \numLeaves t\rceil < \depth(\eval t\ X.\mathrm{add})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $X = W$, $t = \mathrm{caterpillar}\,0\,3$: depth $= 3$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:cateval}),
$\maxLeafDepth = 0$, $\numLeaves = 4$, $\clog 4\rceil = 2$, and $0 + 2 < 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential reassociation gap]\label{thm:gap}
On $\mathrm{unitCostAdd}$, a balanced tree on $2^n$ leaves has depth $n$, while a
caterpillar on $2^n$ leaves has depth $2^n - 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:baleval} gives $n$; a caterpillar with $2^n$ leaves has height
$2^n - 1$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:cateval} gives $2^n - 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{C4/C5: composition and Hensel lifting}

A \emph{composition carrier} attaches a $\depth$ to maps with $\op = (\circ)$
obeying $\depth(f \circ g) \le \max(\depth f, \depth g) + 1$; it is a depth
carrier.

\begin{theorem}[Composition bound]\label{thm:comp}
For a composition tree $t$,
$\depth(\eval t\ (\circ)) \le \maxLeafDepth\,\depth\,t + \height t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact balanced composition]\label{thm:compeq}
Balanced composition of $2^n$ maps each of depth $d$ has depth exactly $d + n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} plus Lemma~\ref{lem:baleval} on the composition carrier
with constant leaf depth $d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hensel certificate]\label{thm:hensel}
In the Hensel depth carrier, the $k$-fold balanced doubling tree of height $k$
has depth exactly $k$ and certifies $p$-adic precision exactly $2^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Depth $= k$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:baleval} (start depth $0$). Precision solves the
doubling recurrence $\mathrm{prec}(k{+}1) = 2\,\mathrm{prec}(k)$,
$\mathrm{prec}(0) = 1$, giving $2^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exponential-precision lifting]\label{cor:fast}
To reach target precision $T$, the number of doubling rounds is $\clog T\rceil$,
recovering exponential precision in logarithmically many rounds.
\end{corollary}

\section{C3: the strict regime}

\begin{theorem}[Zero height overhead]\label{thm:strict}
If $X$ is strict, then for every tree $t$,
$\depth(\eval t\ X.\mathrm{add}) \le \maxLeafDepth\,\depth\,t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The induction of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} with the strict law (no $+1$): at each
node $\depth(\eval l \op \eval r) \le \max(\depth\eval l, \depth\eval r) \le
\maxLeafDepth t$, so the height term never appears.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A1: evaluate a tree.} Fold bottom-up; time $O(\numLeaves)$, recursion
depth $O(\height)$. With $\mathrm{unitCostAdd}$ and equal leaves $b$, output
$b + \height(t)$.

\paragraph{A2: optimal rebalancing.} From $m$ leaves build a balanced tree of
height $\clog m\rceil$, minimizing evaluated depth and restoring the logarithmic
bound (Theorem~\ref{thm:balbound}).

\paragraph{A3: Hensel doubling schedule.} For target precision $T$, run
$k = \clog T\rceil$ balanced doubling rounds; precision after round $j$ is $2^j$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:hensel}, Corollary~\ref{cor:fast}).

\section{Applications and discussion}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is a statement about \emph{critical-path length}: parallel
reduction time is tree height, not total work, and Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} is the
formal reason reductions must be balanced. Section~7 places fast Hensel lifting,
$p$-adic root-finding, and Newton high-precision arithmetic under the same bound.
The unit-cost law is an ultrametric triangle inequality, so combinatorial bounds
on tree shapes transfer through the valuation-depth $\to$ tropical functor to
certified radii in nonarchimedean metrics, with the now-pinned constant $1$.

\section{Future work}

(D1) Construct a left adjoint $\mathrm{Strictify}$ to the inclusion of strict
carriers, via depth saturation, with a 1-Lipschitz universal unit
(Theorem~\ref{thm:strict} supplies the defining inequality). (D2) Upgrade
Corollary~\ref{cor:const} to equality on extremal carriers, proving the balanced
tree uniquely minimizes evaluated depth on a fixed multiset of equal leaves.
Sharpen C1 for associative-commutative carriers, complete the $(\circ)$
functoriality of C4, and instantiate the concrete Hensel carrier of C5.

\section{Conclusion}

A single unit-cost ultrametric inequality, lifted to combination trees, yields
one law: the depth of a combined object is its starting depth plus the height of
the combination---never directly its size. The unit toll is intrinsically forced,
the height dependence is sharp, and the same arithmetic governs tropical
valuation growth, composition depth, and Hensel precision doubling. In every
register, \textbf{the only cost is height}.

\end{document}
